1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Look at the width on this. Six hundred pages. Ten hours minimum.

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Every feed keeps handing you another trilogy. Today, one rule.

3
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:38,000
Under two hundred and twenty pages. Finish it before Monday.

4
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Most people think short means light.

5
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Not here. When you hit a slump, three questions cut the pile in half.

6
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Question one: pacing or atmosphere? You want a page-turner that starts moving on page four.

7
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Watch what happens when we throw out the slow setup.

8
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Pick one is [PICK 1 TITLE - CONFIRM]. Look at the spacing on this opening page.

9
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Zero prologue. You meet the trouble by line six. Two breaths in, you're already late for bed.

10
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Question two: what if you hate epic quests and want tight tension in one room? Then we shrink the room.

11
00:02:51,000 --> 00:03:10,000
[PICK 2 TITLE - CONFIRM]. One decision, one weekend, zero filler chapters.

12
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:24,000
You read this one with your shoulders up.

13
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Last question. What surprises someone who thinks they only read nonfiction? [PICK 3 TITLE - CONFIRM].

14
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:58,000
True story. Shorter than your commute playlist.

15
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Look up all three in the Calgary Public Library catalogue—check live availability at your branch or place a hold right from home.

16
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Now it's your turn. Drop your weirdest one-line constraint in the comments. "I liked X, I'm sick of Y, give me Z."

17
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Next episode starts with one of yours right here on this table.
